# Coding Standards

## No emojis in code

Never put emojis in source code, comments, log messages, commit messages, or CLI
output. Plain text only.

## Simplest thing that works

Build only what the current task needs.

- No abstraction layers, interfaces, or config options with a single use.
- No frameworks or dependencies when a few lines of standard library do the job.
- No speculative features, hooks, or "we might need this later" parameters.
- Prefer a plain function over a class, a class over a hierarchy.

If a solution needs a diagram to explain, it is probably too complex.

## Comments explain thinking, not code

Code should be readable enough that most functions need no comment at all. Write a
comment only to capture something the code cannot show: a non-obvious constraint, a
tradeoff, a workaround for external behavior, or why an obvious approach was rejected.

```python
# BAD - restates the code
# increment the counter by one
counter += 1

# GOOD - explains hidden logic
# The API returns 429 without Retry-After, so back off on a fixed schedule.
time.sleep(BACKOFF_SECONDS)
```

No docstrings that repeat the signature, no section banners, no commented-out code.
When a comment is needed to explain what a block does, rename things or extract a
function instead.
